Usages:
  range_permission_cache: |
    Point and range permission checks (IsPutPermitted, IsRangePermitted, IsDeleteRangePermitted)
    are answered from a per-user interval-tree cache built by merging every role a user holds,
    rebuilt whenever auth configuration changes (user/role grant/revoke, enable/disable). Add
    new permission-affecting operations to the same rebuild path rather than checking role
    permission lists directly on the request path.

Annotations: |
  This cell owns etcd's authentication/authorization domain: users, roles, permission grants,
  token issuance/validation (with pluggable simple/JWT/no-op strategies), and the authorization
  gate consulted before every key-value mutation and read. It defines its own narrow backend
  abstraction for persistence rather than importing a storage cell directly, keeping it usable
  independent of which concrete storage implementation backs it.

  Use `range_permission_cache` when adding a new permission-checking entry point.

---

"AuthStore(tokenProvider TokenProvider, bcryptCost int)":
  location: store.go
  annotations: |
    The single authorization gate: enable/disable auth, manage users/roles/permissions, issue
    and validate tokens, and answer per-request permission checks.

    `tokenProvider`: the token issuance/validation strategy in effect (simple, JWT, or no-op).
    `bcryptCost`: hashing cost for stored user passwords.
  methods:
    "AuthEnable() -> err:error": |
      Turn on the authentication feature; from this point, unauthenticated/under-privileged
      requests are rejected.
    "AuthDisable()": |
      Turn off the authentication feature.
    "Authenticate(username string, password string) -> response:AuthenticateResponse, err:error": |
      Verify a username/password pair and, on success, issue a new token.
    "UserAdd(request UserAddRequest) -> response:UserAddResponse, err:error": |
      Create a new user with a bcrypt-hashed password.
    "UserGrantRole(request UserGrantRoleRequest) -> response:UserGrantRoleResponse, err:error": |
      Grant an existing role to an existing user, then rebuild that user's entry in the
      `range_permission_cache`.
    "RoleAdd(request RoleAddRequest) -> response:RoleAddResponse, err:error": |
      Create a new, initially permission-less role.
    "RoleGrantPermission(request RoleGrantPermissionRequest) -> response:RoleGrantPermissionResponse, err:error": |
      Grant a read/write/read-write permission over a key or key range to a role, then rebuild
      the `range_permission_cache` for every user holding that role.
    "IsPutPermitted(info AuthInfo, key []byte) -> err:error": |
      Check whether the authenticated caller described by `info` may write to `key`.
    "IsRangePermitted(info AuthInfo, key []byte, rangeEnd []byte) -> err:error": |
      Check whether the authenticated caller described by `info` may read the range from `key`
      up to (excluding) `rangeEnd`.
    "IsAdminPermitted(info AuthInfo) -> err:error": |
      Check whether the authenticated caller described by `info` holds the root role.
    "GenTokenPrefix() -> prefix:string, err:error": |
      Produce a fresh random prefix for the next issued simple token; a no-op returning an
      empty string when the JWT strategy is in effect.
    "Recover(be AuthBackend)": |
      Reload all auth state from `be` after a snapshot has replaced the backend wholesale.

"TokenProvider(strategy string, ttl Duration)":
  location: simple_token.go
  annotations: |
    Issues and validates opaque authentication tokens. Exactly one of three interchangeable
    strategies is active at a time: a random-string token backed by an in-memory TTL keeper
    ("simple"), a signed/verified JWT requiring no server-side state ("jwt"), or a no-op
    strategy that always rejects token issuance (used when no token option is configured).

    `strategy`: which of the three implementations to construct.
    `ttl`: token validity duration for the simple strategy.

